data.content:
Your code **doesn't use `Thread.Abort`**, so there's nothing to migrate on that front. It uses `Thread.Start`/`Join`, which still work in .NET 8.

However, the code has a **closure bug** — the `item` variable is captured by reference, so all threads may process the *last* item. This was fixed in C# 5+ (`foreach` now scopes per iteration), but it's worth noting.

**Recommended modernization** — replace raw threads with `Parallel.ForEach` or `Task`:

```csharp
public class SimpleWorker
{
    public void RunBatch(List<WorkItem> items)
    {
        Parallel.ForEach(items, item =>
        {
            Process(item);
        });
    }

    private void Process(WorkItem item)
    {
        item.Result = ComputeResult(item.Input);
    }
}
```

**Thread.Abort migration cheat-sheet** (for when you do encounter it):

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 replacement |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationToken` + cooperative check |
| `Thread.Abort()` for timeout | `Task.Run` + `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter` |
| `try/catch(ThreadAbortException)` | `catch(OperationCanceledException)` |

`Thread.Abort` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 8 — there is no direct replacement; all cancellation must be **cooperative**.
